{"ask":"audit_q_human_reconcile","at":1548,"kind":"fork","lane":"audit","of_turn":2,"question":"We're at a phase boundary — the operator declared one, so I want to audit our working notes (working notes). The operator changed some of them since the last boundary: a note marked [edited by the operator] is their correction, and a note marked [flagged: …] carries their concern. For each note below, reply with EXACTLY one line, each starting with its number:\n<n>. KEEP\nor <n>. UPDATE: <the corrected one-line note>\nor <n>. REMOVE — <one-clause reason>\nor <n>. REMOVE — dup of #<k>  (when it duplicates another note here)\nAddress every marked note: KEEP an edited note — it stands as the operator wrote it — and UPDATE or REMOVE any note of yours it makes wrong; KEEP a flagged note if the concern does not hold, or UPDATE or REMOVE it if it does. Judge against everything that has actually happened in this session. Output 18 lines and nothing else.\n\n1. learned: The checker's success path works — with every local link resolving it prints nothing and exits 0 — and the docs tree contained no other broken links, i.e. the fence-skip and external-scheme-skip logic produced no false positives on the fake in-fence link and the https link.\n2. evidence: The run printed only `exit=0` after `touch docs/missing.md`; this depended on the prior run having reported exactly one broken link (`docs/index.md:4: missing.md`) and on `check_file`'s anchor/external/fence skips being correct, since any of those failing would have added report lines and broken the exit-0 result.\n3. learned: The checker's success path is verified — with no broken links it prints nothing and exits 0 (the `missing.md` target counts as existing as soon as the file is present, since the check is a plain existence test).\n4. evidence: The run output was empty except `exit=0`, immediately after `touch docs/missing.md` and before `rm` restored the broken state.\n5. learned: linkcheck.py detects the seeded broken link, prints it as `file:line: target`, exits 1 when broken links exist, and exits 0 when the target file exists (also correctly ignoring external links and fenced code).\n6. evidence: first run printed `docs/index.md:4: missing.md` with `exit=1`; after `touch docs/missing.md` the second run printed nothing with `exit=0`.\n7. plan: Nothing further is intended; no follow-up steps unless the user requests changes (e.g., new options, CI integration, or additional fixtures).\n8. learned: linkcheck.py's success path works — it exits 0 when every local link resolves, and the broken `missing.md` link at docs/index.md:4 was the only broken link in docs/ (fenced code, external, and anchor links were all correctly ignored).\n9. evidence: With `touch docs/missing.md` in place, the run printed only `exit=0`; the identical run without it had printed `docs/index.md:4: missing.md` and `exit=1` — the only difference between the two runs was the file's existence.\n10. learned: linkcheck.py now reports broken links as JSON Lines — one object per line with keys file, line, target — replacing the earlier plain-text output decision, with exit codes unchanged (1 broken, 0 clean, 2 bad root). [flagged: stale?]\n11. evidence: Running `python3 linkcheck.py` printed `{\"file\": \"docs/index.md\", \"line\": 4, \"target\": \"missing.md\"}` on a single line and exited 1.\n12. replaced by this turn's DECISION (output format is JSON Lines, not `file:line: target`).\n13. learned: The script's output format is now JSON Lines (one object per line with keys file, line, target), established by `check_file` appending dicts to `broken` and `main` printing `json.dumps(entry)` per entry; exit codes (1 broken / 0 clean / 2 bad root) were kept as-is.\n14. evidence: `python3 linkcheck.py` printed `{\"file\": \"docs/index.md\", \"line\": 4, \"target\": \"missing.md\"}` and `exit=1`, matching the expected single broken link in `docs/index.md`.\n15. learned: The checker now reports broken links as JSON Lines (one object per line with keys file, line, target) while keeping the exit code; it depends on check_file appending dicts whose values are JSON-serializable strings, and on docs/index.md actually referencing a missing target for the run to exercise the broken-link path.\n16. evidence: Running `python3 linkcheck.py` printed `{\"file\": \"docs/index.md\", \"line\": 4, \"target\": \"missing.md\"}` and exited with code 1.\n17. decision: Broken links are now reported as JSON Lines — one object per line with keys file, line and target — while the exit code stays (1 if any broken, 0 clean, 2 bad root); implemented in linkcheck.py (check_file appends dicts, main prints json.dumps per entry) and verified by one run: `{\"file\": \"docs/index.md\", \"line\": 4, \"target\": \"missing.md\"}` with exit=1.\n18. plan: Nothing further unless the user requests changes (e.g., pretty-printing, different keys, or CI integration).","seq":2310,"t":95904,"why":"meditation"}
